WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Can you go go deeper on your this

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.600
attribute attribute of winners in the

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.760
fact that the first one is they have an

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.360
abundance mindset?

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.280
I think there's there's two there's two

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.920
types of people.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.520
The first type and the less effective

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.960
type wants to be the biggest fish in the

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.160
small pond. They want to have the

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.280
biggest house in the neighborhood. They

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.720
want to have the nicest car at the

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.120
country club. They're constantly

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.640
comparing themselves to other people.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.880
Then there's the other side who says

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.800
hey man, like there's enough out there

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.480
for everybody and if I can live next to

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.720
people and be around people and be in

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.680
the same circles with people who are

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.200
doing better than I am, that doesn't

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:44.280
upset me. I don't get jealous of that.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.920
It rubs off on me and the opportunities

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.560
flow. So what you don't understand about

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.760
your friends making money and kicking

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.120
ass is it doesn't make you look bad. It

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.280
actually makes it more likely that

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.055
you're going to make more money and kick

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.480
more ass.

00:00:53.055 --> 00:00:54.880
>> [laughter]

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.800
>> A lot of people have a lot of people

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.400
have this

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.200
crabs in a bucket mentality where

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.080
you look at crabs in a bucket, one of

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.120
them tries to get out, the other one

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.480
grabs it and pulls it back down because

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.680
and and so none of them actually end up

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.680
getting out of the bucket. There's

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.040
there's a lot of communities and and

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.440
friend groups and cultures

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.760
that

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.040
that's the mindset. That's the mindset.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.480
Somebody winning is bad for me because

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.680
there's only X amount of money, there's

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.120
only X amount of opportunity, there's

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.240
only X amount of success. So if they

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.640
have it, that's something that I don't

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.640
have, I'm pissed.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.280
When in reality there's there's enough

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.840
money for

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.640
everybody to get theirs. You just got to

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.240
add enough value to the world. Oh,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.840
absolutely. And part part of that um

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.640
that flows I think with that is

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.920
something that you learned early on from

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.920
one of your mentors, okay?

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.600
Um can you talk to me about your initial

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.200
conversation with Dan Cohen

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.626
um specifically when it relates to

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.920
sales?

00:01:54.626 --> 00:01:57.960
>> [laughter]

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.120
>> This is another uh

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.320
you know, I took a I took a piece of

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.880
humble pie during this conversation. Um

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.280
I was

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.759
a senior. I just had my first you know,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.000
$3,000 successful storage season and I

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.360
was on top of the world and I wanted to

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.200
be an entrepreneur and I was into it. I

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.080
was starting to read about

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.240
entrepreneurship and study

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.920
entrepreneurship. And I

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.880
I call my mentor Dan Cohen who's now

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.440
at Wake Forest running the

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.600
entrepreneurship program there. He was

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.720
at Cornell.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.280
He had sold a foundation repair business

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.160
for you know,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.720
eight almost eight figures um

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.760
couple years earlier. So

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.840
sweaty startup, not a tech startup.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.800
And he's like, let's get lunch. I was

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.880
like, I want to pick your brain. I want

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.920
to get lunch. So I get lunch with him

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.880
and he said we sit down at lunch and he

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.959
goes,

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.040
"Hey Nick, I got a question for you."

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:41.400
First thing, we hadn't even ordered yet.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.840
He's like,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.480
"Do you like sales? What do you think of

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.720
sales? Do you like it?"

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.991
And I knew by the way he said it that I

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.200
needed to say I love sales.

00:02:48.991 --> 00:02:50.400
>> [laughter]

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.000
>> But I was on I was honest with him. I

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.080
said, "No, um I don't like sales. It's

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.280
uncomfortable. I don't want to have

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.480
uncomfortable conversations. I don't

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.720
like rejection. I'm a visionary. I'm an

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.280
entrepreneur. I'm on the whiteboard

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.400
thinking about ways to improve. I'm

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.280
building processes. I'm you know,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.320
execution." And I said all of this all

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.440
these entrepreneurial buzzwords.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.120
And he looks at me and he goes,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:17.920
"Nick, you need to go get a job."

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.200
And I'm like, "What?" And he goes, "If

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.920
you don't like sales, you need to go get

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.840
a job."

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.959
Because not only is every single bit of

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.400
entrepreneurship sales,

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:28.920
life is sales. And if you don't like

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.040
sales, you're not going to get things

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:33.160
you want in life.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.000
From the wife you want to marry to the

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.640
friends you want to hang out with to it

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.280
to the getting your kids to do what you

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.200
need them to do

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.800
all the way to, okay, if we zoom in on

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.720
entrepreneurship,

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:47.239
as a beginning of a fresh entrepreneur,

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.160
you have to sell people to work for you

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:51.239
because they're taking a risk.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.600
You have to sell people to sell to you

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:53.920
because you don't have credit or the

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.360
money. They

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.440
I literally was calling box companies

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.120
and warehouse companies selling them on

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.640
me because I was a 23-year-old

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.440
snotty-nosed kid running around with no

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.600
ca- no cash trying to lease their 25,000

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.959
square foot warehouse in downtown

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.080
Boston. I had to put my sales head on

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:10.600
and convince that owner to lease that

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.720
warehouse to me.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:15.240
And then I had to sell my customers.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.480
Man, it was it was sales nonstop. So, he

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.160
told me that. He said, "Look, if you

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.200
don't like sales,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.000
you're in trouble, A.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:24.240
But luckily, you can train yourself to

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.600
love sales. So, you need to become a

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.200
good salesman now. Tell yourself you're

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.040
a good salesperson. And the way to do it

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.080
is to practice and get used to

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.600
rejection. And so, I've spent the last

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.040
15 years

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.240
going from

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.080
you know,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.440
imagine that kid in his in the kitchen

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.200
who's afraid to call the restaurant to

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.480
set the reservation to uh

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.280
having uncomfortable conversations all

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:48.480
day because that's what you do as the

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.480
leader of of a business.

